<hr>
<h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Choose the correct answer from the given choices.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> The diagram shows the stomatal apparatus of a green leaf. What causes the opening of stomatal pores during photosynthesis?</div><div style='background:#fef08a;color:#854d0e;padding:6px 12px;border-radius:4px;display:inline-block;font-weight:bold;margin:6px 0;'>[IMAGE NEEDED: NCERT Diagram: stomata, guard cells, stomatal pore]</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Swelling of guard cells due to influx of water</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Shrinkage of guard cells due to loss of water</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) High concentration of oxygen in guard cells</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Movement of chloroplasts away from the pore</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Which of the following statements is correct regarding the anaerobic breakdown of pyruvate in yeast cells?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Pyruvate breaks down into ethanol and carbon dioxide in the cytoplasm.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Pyruvate breaks down into lactic acid and energy in mitochondria.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Pyruvate breaks down into carbon dioxide and water in mitochondria.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Pyruvate breaks down into glucose and oxygen in the cytoplasm.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> Which component of gastric juice protects the inner lining of the stomach from the harmful action of hydrochloric acid?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Pepsin</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Mucus</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Trypsin</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Bile juice</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> Deoxygenated blood from the body enters which chamber of the human heart first?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Left atrium</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Left ventricle</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Right atrium</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Right ventricle</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> Identify the primary site of filtration in the structure of a nephron.</div><div style='background:#fef08a;color:#854d0e;padding:6px 12px;border-radius:4px;display:inline-block;font-weight:bold;margin:6px 0;'>[IMAGE NEEDED: NCERT Diagram: nephron, glomerulus, bowmans capsule]</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Loop of Henle</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Collecting duct</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Glomerulus inside Bowman's capsule</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Tubular part of nephron</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q6.</strong> Which plant hormone promotes cell division and is present in high concentration in fruits and seeds?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Auxin</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Gibberellin</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Cytokinin</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Abscisic acid</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q7.</strong> Which part of the human brain is responsible for maintaining posture and balance of the body?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Cerebrum</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Cerebellum</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Medulla</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Pons</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q8.</strong> In a neuron, conversion of an electrical signal to a chemical signal occurs at/in:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Cell body</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Axonal end / Synapse</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Dendritic tip</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Axon membrane</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q9.</strong> Which among the following organisms reproduces by multiple fission?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Amoeba</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Leishmania</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Plasmodium</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Paramecium</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q10.</strong> In a flower, the post-fertilization changes lead to the development of ovary into _____ and ovules into _____.</div><div style='background:#fef08a;color:#854d0e;padding:6px 12px;border-radius:4px;display:inline-block;font-weight:bold;margin:6px 0;'>[IMAGE NEEDED: NCERT Diagram: flower, carpel, ovary, ovule]</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) seed, fruit</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) fruit, seed</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) flower, fruit</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) embryo, seed</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q11.</strong> Which surgical method of contraception prevents sperms from reaching the urethra in human males?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Tubectomy</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Vasectomy</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Copper-T insertion</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Oral pills</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q12.</strong> When a pure tall pea plant (TT) is crossed with a pure dwarf pea plant (tt), the phenotypic ratio in the F<sub>2</sub> generation will be:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 1 : 2 : 1</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 3 : 1</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 9 : 3 : 3 : 1</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 2 : 1</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q13.</strong> What determines the sex of a child in human beings?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) The type of sex chromosome present in the egg from the mother</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) The type of sex chromosome present in the sperm from the father</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) The nutrition level of the mother during pregnancy</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) The age of the parents at the time of fertilization</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q14.</strong> According to Lindeman's 10% law, if 10,000 J of energy is available at the producer level in an ecosystem, how much energy will be transferred to the secondary consumer?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 1000 J</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 100 J</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 10 J</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 1 J</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q15.</strong> Depletion of the ozone layer in the upper atmosphere is mainly attributed to the release of:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>)</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Methane (CH<sub>4</sub>)</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Sulfur dioxide (SO<sub>2</sub>)</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q16.</strong> Which of the following is a non-biodegradable waste product?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Vegetable peels</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Paper bags</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Polythene bags</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Animal manure</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q17.</strong> The correct path of a reflex arc involved in reacting to a hot stimulus is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Receptor -> Motor neuron -> Spinal cord -> Sensory neuron -> Effector</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Receptor -> Sensory neuron -> Spinal cord -> Motor neuron -> Effector</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Effector -> Sensory neuron -> Brain -> Motor neuron -> Receptor</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Sensory neuron -> Receptor -> Brain -> Effector -> Motor neuron</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section B: Assertion-Reason</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: For questions 18 to 20, two statements are given—one labelled Assertion (A) and the other labelled Reason (R). Select the correct answer from the options given below:
(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.
(b) Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.
(c) A is true but R is false.
(d) A is false but R is true.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q18.</strong> Assertion (A): Plants have much lower energy needs compared to animals.\nReason (R): Plants do not move and plant bodies have a large proportion of dead cells in many tissues.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A is true but R is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A is false but R is true.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q19.</strong> Assertion (A): An aquarium requires regular cleaning and maintenance, whereas a natural pond does not.\nReason (R): An aquarium is an artificial ecosystem that lacks natural decomposers to process organic waste.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A is true but R is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A is false but R is true.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q20.</strong> Assertion (A): Testes in human males are located outside the abdominal cavity within the scrotum.\nReason (R): Sperm production requires a temperature 2 to 2.5 °C lower than the normal human body temperature.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A is true but R is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A is false but R is true.</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section C: Very Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions briefly.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q21.</strong> What is translocation in plants? Name the vascular tissue responsible for this process and state the form in which food is transported.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q22.</strong> State two fundamental differences between autotrophic nutrition and heterotrophic nutrition.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q23.</strong> Define reflex action. Give one suitable example from daily life.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q24.</strong> Name two plant growth hormones (phytohormones) and specify one main function for each.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q25.</strong> Differentiate between binary fission and multiple fission with one example for each.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q26.</strong> What is placenta? State two essential functions performed by the placenta during pregnancy in human females.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q27.</strong> What are homologous organs? Give one example to explain how they provide evidence for evolution.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q28.</strong> Why is the flow of energy in an ecosystem considered unidirectional? Explain briefly.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q29.</strong> Define biological magnification. Name the trophic level that accumulates the maximum concentration of toxic chemicals in a food chain.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q30.</strong> State two adverse effects of ozone layer depletion on human health and the environment.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section D: Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions in detail.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q31.</strong> Describe the pathway of blood circulation through the human heart. Why is human blood circulation called double circulation?</div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFWM0AAAADUlEQVR4nGNgAAAAAgABSK+kcQAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q32.</strong> Explain the step-by-step mechanism of urine formation in a human nephron under three headings: (a) Ultrafiltration, (b) Selective reabsorption, and (c) Tubular secretion.</div><div style='background:#fef08a;color:#854d0e;padding:6px 12px;border-radius:4px;display:inline-block;font-weight:bold;margin:6px 0;'>[IMAGE NEEDED: NCERT Diagram: nephron structure, bowmans capsule, kidney tubule]</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q33.</strong> How does the movement in leaves of a sensitive plant (Mimosa pudica) differ from the movement of shoots towards light? Give three points of distinction.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q34.</strong> Name the hormone secreted by each of the following endocrine glands and state one major function of each:
(a) Thyroid gland
(b) Pancreas
(c) Adrenal gland</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q35.</strong> Explain the process of pollination and fertilization in a flowering plant. What is double fertilization?</div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFWM0AAAADUlEQVR4nGNgAAAAAgABSK+kcQAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q36.</strong> A pure tall pea plant (TT) with red flowers (RR) is crossed with a dwarf pea plant (tt) with white flowers (rr). Write the genotype and phenotype of the F<sub>1</sub> progeny. What will be the phenotypic ratio in the F<sub>2</sub> generation?</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q37.</strong> Explain sex determination in human beings with the help of a flow chart/cross diagram. Why are mothers not responsible for the sex of the child?</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q38.</strong> Differentiate between aerobic and anaerobic respiration on the basis of:
(a) Presence or absence of oxygen
(b) End products formed in animal cells/yeast
(c) Amount of energy released per molecule of glucose</div>
</body>
</html>